            <ArticleTitle>Hereditary angioedema first apparent in the ninth decade during treatment with ACE inhibitor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>138-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Hereditary angioedema ordinarily manifests itself in childhood. The development of angioedema in this disease and as a side effect of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor treatment is caused by similar mechanisms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of female patient, diagnosed with hereditary angioedema at age 90, who experienced the first attack 8 years earlier during angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This patient presented with her first episode of hereditary angioedema at the oldest age ever reported. A careful family history, especially for angioedema, should be taken in patients of all ages before initiating treatment with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
